<section xml:id="sec_eq-graphs" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  	<title>Equipotential Graphs</title>
		
    <corollary xml:id="vid-eq-graphs">
	<title>Equipotential Graphs</title>
	
	<statement>
		<p>
			
		</p>

		<video youtube="6frqYEcoLiE"><description>An introduction to equipotential graphs.</description></video>

	</statement>
  </corollary>

  <subsubsection><title>Key Ideas</title></subsubsection>
	
	<p>Similar to contour or topographical maps, equipotential graphs used curved lines to illustrate the value of the electric potential in space.</p>	
				
	<conjecture xml:id="rep_eq-graphs">
		<title>Equipotential Graphs</title>
		<statement>
			<p>
				
			</p>
			<p>An equipotential graph uses contour lines to show the electric potential.</p>
				<ul>
					<li>
						<p>
							<alert>The potential is constant along a line.</alert> Each line corresponds to a single value of the potential.
						</p>
					</li>
					<li>
						<p>
							<alert>Adjacent lines are separated by the same voltage.</alert> The separation between adjacent lines can be any number of volts, but it must be the same for the entire graph.
						</p>
					</li>
					<li>
						<p>
							<alert>Equipotentials are three-dimensional.</alert> The electric potential is a scalar field of all three spatial dimensions.  Equipotential surfaces can be hard to draw, so it is common to represent cross-sections as equipotential lines in two dimensions.
						</p>
					</li>
					<li>
						<p>
							<alert>The electric field points perpendicular to the equipotentials.</alert> The magnitude of the electric field can be estimated by looking at the local density of the equipotential lines.
						</p>
					</li>
				</ul>
				<figure xml:id="fig_EQgraph0">
			  <title>Example Equipotential Graph</title>
			<caption><alert>Example:</alert> Five equipotential lines for a positive point charge located at the origin.</caption>
			<image source="EQgraph0.png" width = "100%" ><description>An xy contour graph showing 5 concentric circles, labeled from outside to inside: 18 V, 27 V, 36 V, 45 V, 54 V.</description></image>
		</figure>
		</statement>
	</conjecture>

	<subsubsection>
		<title>Activities</title>
		<investigation>
      <title>Moving through Equipotentials</title>
	  <introduction>
      <p></p>
			<p>Consider the equipotential graph shown below.</p>
				<figure xml:id="eq">
			  <title>Equipotential Graph</title>
			<caption>An equipotential graph in a region of space. The numbers indicate the value of the electric potential for the corresponding line. </caption>
			<image source="EQgraph1.png" width = "100%" ><description>An equipotential graph in a region of space. The graph shows a region of space, x vs. y, and 12 equipotential lines.</description></image>
		</figure>
		</introduction>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Suppose you are initially located at <m>x = 0</m> and <m>y = -1 \mathrm{~m}</m>. Describe how the electric potential changes as you walk in the positive <m>y</m>-direction toward the top of the graph.  Explain your reasoning.
				</p>
			</statement>
		</task>
      <task>
	  <statement>
			<p>Describe how the magnitude of the electric field <m>|\vec{E}|</m> changes as you walk in the positive <m>y</m>-direction toward the top of the graph.  Explain your reasoning.</p>
		</statement>
		</task>
		</investigation>
	</subsubsection>
	
<!--NO AI-->
</section>

